    "knowledge_point": "Solving an Inequality When the Base of the Exponential is Greater Than One",
    "problem": "Solve the inequality $2{e}^{x-2}+1<3.$",
    "worked_solution": "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{x-2}+1 & <3 \\\\ 2{e}^{x-2} & <2 \\\\ {e}^{x-2} & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-2}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡1 \\\\ x-2 & <0 \\\\ x & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
